  "narrative_body": "The corruption trial of José Luis Ábalos, the former transport minister, began this week with witnesses describing cash payments and envelopes of money being handled at PSOE headquarters. Multiple witnesses testified about systematic cash distribution through party offices, with family members collecting money on behalf of officials.\n\nThe trial represents the most serious judicial threat to Pedro Sánchez's government since taking office. Mr Ábalos served as PSOE's organisational secretary before joining the cabinet, making the testimony about cash handling at party headquarters particularly damaging for the ruling socialists.\n\nEven as PSOE faces corruption charges, the far-right Vox party is tearing itself apart. Expelled members including Javier Ortega Smith accused Santiago Abascal of being in politics \"por la pasta\" — for the money — and personal enrichment. The crisis has spread across multiple regional organisations, with leadership purges and accusations of authoritarian management.\n\nMeanwhile, Spain's regional politics are heating up in Andalusia, where the campaign has national implications. The PP's Juanma Moreno is seeking an absolute majority by warning against \"chaos,\" while PSOE's María Jesús Montero is promising healthcare reforms. The contest will test the governing party's strength amid the corruption allegations.\n\nSpain reacted predictably to Viktor Orbán's electoral defeat in Hungary this week. Mr Sánchez and PP leader Alberto Núñez Feijóo celebrated the victory as pro-European, while Mr Abascal lamented the loss of his ally, calling Orbán's Hungary \"the only nation in Europe saved from Islamist invasion.\" Mr Sánchez also called on the EU to suspend its association agreement with Israel following intensified bombing in Lebanon.\n\nDespite the political turbulence, Spain's economic fundamentals remain strong. Spanish household wealth reached a record €2.645 trillion in 2025, up 11% from the previous year. Household debt fell to 42.8% of GDP — the lowest level in 25 years. The Bank of Spain data shows Spanish families are richer and less indebted than they have been since 1999.\n\nIn routine institutional business, Cristina Navarro Enterría was appointed as the new CNIO managing director, becoming the first woman to hold the position. The appointment comes during a corruption investigation into alleged financial irregularities at the intelligence centre.",
